#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506608} :activity/id #uuid "0fada08e-9ee8-50a7-a281-27eecc4451e2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049068 :topic/id #uuid "d78ca6bb-945f-51db-a4af-fc777024df22" :topic/math-academy-id 1459} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04cd6f91-4830-5fb2-a760-6d1458397aff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505925} {:answer/id #uuid "3cb14bc8-1566-5a66-a92e-6847a87ef3a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506011} {:answer/id #uuid "40c8a717-049b-5524-b555-5e7634dfb774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506019} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6d04c0-fedf-5f99-93ce-81d660f42b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506097} {:answer/id #uuid "9f2a5be0-7fd3-5d96-a0c9-2797fdcde248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506011} :answer-field/id #uuid "b73d6aaf-8b71-5986-9a72-f3f8cba064c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "16005fe9-c0e8-5807-8dbf-172a83d89558" :question/math-academy-id "q-53062" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. A square is sometimes not a rectangle.\n\nII. A rectangle is always a quadrilateral.\n\nIII. A square is never a rhombus." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. In a rectangle, its four angles are right angles. The same is true for a square. A square is a specific type of rectangle, where the four angles are right angles and the four sides are congruent.\n\n- Statement II is true. A rectangle is always a quadrilateral because it has four sides.\n\n- Statement III is false. A rhombus is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. A square is a specific type of rhombus, where the four angles are right angles and the four sides are congruent.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."} :step/id #uuid "1472f937-314f-5429-8263-e1a67f2a5b4d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506644}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "015f0057-b143-57ad-8fa5-2978f72ea3dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505914} {:answer/id #uuid "1afb074a-342e-57b7-b473-fffc06924988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505957} {:answer/id #uuid "555289bb-109d-55d5-955e-bed2ff4c8443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506057} {:answer/id #uuid "92f03582-9a2a-59ce-8ac4-d6529d374b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506160} {:answer/id #uuid "e905ed52-46a6-5b4b-9ca4-e3a4bea9ebdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506337}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506160} :answer-field/id #uuid "38a2df37-5f07-59b3-9c74-e390691ea42b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "978998ae-c2d1-5709-a415-cc49252cea0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-53063" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. A parallelogram is sometimes a rectangle.\n\nII. A kite is never a quadrilateral.\n\nIII. A square is always a trapezoid." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. A parallelogram is a rectangle when all the angles are right.\n\n- Statement II is false. A kite is always a quadrilateral because it has four sides.\n\n- Statement III is false. A trapezoid has only $1$ pair of opposite sides that are parallel. However, in a square, $2$ pairs of opposite sides are parallel, and so doesn't fit the definition of a trapezoid.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."} :step/id #uuid "a7b19881-e4d4-5ce2-a2db-ec80491290b8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506648}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c5ff65a-e0ad-54f7-95cd-72299fae692b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506101} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8a2580-e988-59db-b88f-bdf851f5dff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506224} {:answer/id #uuid "dabfe65d-e001-5f6f-8eae-84c4f049a38c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506304} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b01984-7aec-5b99-b309-32e1700f88cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506364} {:answer/id #uuid "faa76e22-ab2a-5e2f-b51a-88f71d4a4169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506371}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506364} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c39ae96-03b7-5d75-924b-50c66d2fd7a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4ee12482-99b3-5022-8d03-b6e6abfce44f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74591" :question/problem "![](images/e3/e3708d7723606224e201147b432250dd759022fd7ed275dabebf51935d68d8f8.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a rhombus?" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a rhombus is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is a rectangle. The sides are not all congruent, so it is not a rhombus.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a square. All its sides are congruent and the opposite sides are parallel, so it is a rhombus.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a trapezoid. Since it has only one pair of parallel sides, it is not a rhombus.\n\n- The shape $S$ is an isosceles trapezoid.  Since it has only one pair of parallel sides, it is not a rhombus.\n\nTherefore, shape $Q$ is a rhombus. The shapes $P,$ $R,$ and $S$ are not rhombuses."} :step/id #uuid "b98f15bb-0b73-5666-9f9e-51644b2b10be"}] :activity/title "Classifying Quadrilaterals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505900}]}
